Reverse the sprite plane order in GottliebBoard::decode_gfx so plane_offsets is
LSB-first (matching the already-correct tile layout and the Food Fight fix):
    let planes: [usize; 4] = std::array::from_fn(|p| (3 - p) * quarter * 8);
    // [3q*8, 2q*8, q*8, 0]: bit0 <- ROM quarter 3 (MAME RGN_FRAC(3,4)), etc.
One-line change; add a comment noting decode_gfx is LSB-first vs MAME MSB-first.

IN PROGRESS on control-handling. Components landed and 2 of 8 machines migrated; 6 remain.

DONE:
- ee1f6dd — core/src/core/input.rs: DrainPolicy, RelativeCounter, AxisRange, AnalogAxis + 12 unit tests. Includes set_velocity (deferred here from Area 3).
- 82878f9 — quantum (ClampDrop, no keys). Save format UNCHANGED: quantum's Saveable is hand-written and only ever wrote the counter bytes, so counter()/set_counter() keeps old saves loadable. No version bump needed, contrary to the design's blanket assumption.
- 4389122 — tempest (ClampDrop, 3-per-frame key step). Save format DOES break: TempestSystem derives Saveable, so save_version bumped to 2.

REMAINING: ccastles + missile (Unit), marble (ClampCarry), irobot + starwars + roadrunner (AnalogAxis / Family B).

DESIGN CORRECTION FOUND DURING THE WORK: the three drain policies are not interchangeable in how they treat held keys. Unit (ccastles, missile) applies the key step DIRECTLY to the counter and drains pending separately, so a held key and buffered motion can both land in one tick. ClampCarry/ClampDrop fold keys into the accumulator first. RelativeCounter::update reproduces both; do not "simplify" them into one path.

ALSO: the save-state break is per-machine, not universal. Check whether the machine derives Saveable (break, bump version) or hand-writes it (usually no break, route through counter()/set_counter()).

VERIFICATION RECIPE — rebuild this before touching another machine. Baseline the 8 analog machines BEFORE the change, then re-run after; PNG hashes must match exactly.
For each of ccastles, missile, marble, quantum, tempest, irobot, starwars, roadrunner write a .rhai script:
  m.run_frames(600);
  // for each analog control (see below):
  for i in 0..40 { m.input_relative("<axis>", 3.0); m.run_frames(1); }
  for i in 0..40 { m.input_relative("<axis>", -5.0); m.run_frames(1); }
  m.input_axis("<axis>", 0.75); m.run_frames(20);
  m.input_axis("<axis>", -0.5); m.run_frames(20);
  // for each digital direction control, if any:
  m.input("<dir>", true); m.run_frames(30); m.input("<dir>", false); m.run_frames(10);
  m.run_frames(60);
  m.screenshot("<out>/<machine>.png");
Run with: cargo run -q -p phosphor-script -- run <script> --machine <m> ~/ws/mame-runtime/roms
Analog controls per machine: ccastles/missile/quantum trackball_x,trackball_y; marble p1_trackball_x,p1_trackball_y; tempest spinner; irobot stick_x,stick_y; starwars yoke_x,yoke_y; roadrunner p1_stick_x,p1_stick_y.
Directions used: ccastles p1_left,p1_up; irobot stick_left,stick_up; roadrunner p1_left,p1_up.
The probe was confirmed DETERMINISTIC (identical hashes across repeated runs) before being trusted as a gate — do that check first, or the comparison proves nothing.

PROCESS NOTE: the quantum commit initially landed with a broken test build (its own tests still compared the old u8 fields); caught immediately and amended. Run `cargo test -p phosphor-machines`, not just `cargo build`, after each migration — the machine's own save-state round-trip test is the thing most likely to break.

description:
The dip_banks / dip_bank_value / set_dip_bank_value triple is mechanically derived from which board field holds the DSW byte(s). Single-bank form is identical in pacman.rs (board.dip_switches), donkey_kong.rs (board.dsw0) and donkey_kong_jr.rs; two-bank form in digdug.rs (board.dswa/board.dswb):

  impl DipSwitches for DkongSystem {
      fn dip_banks(&self) -> &static [DipSwitchBank] { DKONG_DIP_BANKS }
      fn dip_bank_value(&self, bank: usize) -> u8 { if bank == 0 { self.board.dsw0 } else { 0 } }
      fn set_dip_bank_value(&mut self, bank: usize, value: u8) { if bank == 0 { self.board.dsw0 = value; } }
  }

Add a macro parameterized by the static bank table and field path(s):
  impl_dip_switches!(DkongSystem, DKONG_DIP_BANKS, board.dsw0);                 // 1 bank
  impl_dip_switches!(DigDugSystem, DIGDUG_DIP_BANKS, board.dswa, board.dswb);   // 2 banks

The DIP TABLES (DipSwitchBank/DipOption/DipChoice literals) stay hand-written — real per-game data, not duplication.

This is obvious delegation and sits within machines/CLAUDE.md:52. New macro goes alongside the existing layer in machines/src/lib.rs.

NOTE ON LINE REFERENCES: the design docs Area 5 refs have drifted ~10-20 lines against the current tree — pacman.rs DipSwitches starts at :251, not the :235-253 the doc cites (and its handle_input is at :244-248, not :223-233). Re-verify each cited site before editing; do not trust the docs Area 5 line numbers.

Gate: cargo test -p phosphor-machines. Verify DIP behavior is unchanged by round-tripping each migrated machines bank values through dip_bank_value/set_dip_bank_value.

Found while doing phosphor-emulator-vy6l (giving every don't-care cycle an
address). These two are cycle-COUNT gaps, not addressing gaps, so they were out
of that issue's scope — nothing silent to fix, just cycles we never spend.

CWAI (0x3C). Ours is: read operand, then 12 pushes, then wait. The hardware
spends two more cycles between the operand read and the pushes — one re-driving
PC, one at $FFFF:

    @m_cc &= read_opcode_arg();
    @dummy_read_opcode_arg(0);
    @dummy_vma(1);
    ... PUSH_REGISTERS ...

INTERRUPT_VECTOR. Ours reads the two vector bytes and nothing else
(`execute_interrupt` cycles 20/21, the path CWAI takes on wake). The hardware
brackets the vector fetch with a /VMA cycle on each side:

    @dummy_vma(1);
    @m_pc.b.h = read_vector(0);
    @m_pc.b.l = read_vector(1);
    @dummy_vma(1);

The full NMI/IRQ/FIRQ/SWI entry sequences DO have those brackets (they are
cycles 14 and 17 of `interrupt_full`, now driving $FFFF) — it is only the
vector-only path used to resume from CWAI that is missing them.

Both are excluded from cross-validation (SYNC and CWAI cannot single-step with
no interrupt source), so nothing catches this today. Fixing it changes CWAI's
cycle count, which is why it wants its own change with its own timing check
rather than riding along.
